public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';
    private static final Integer MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST = 200;

    private final List<Id> accountIds;

    public ErpCustomerSync(List<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Re-query so we send the current committed values, not a stale snapshot.
        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry
            FROM Account
            WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ];

        for (Integer i = 0; i < accounts.size(); i += MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST) {
            Integer toIndex = Math.min(i + MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST, accounts.size());
            List<Account> chunk = new List<Account>();
            for (Integer j = i; j < toIndex; j++) {
                chunk.add(accounts[j]);
            }
            sendChunk(chunk);
        }
    }

    private static void sendChunk(List<Account> accounts) {
        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
        req.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        req.setMethod('POST');
        req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        req.setBody(buildBody(accounts));

        HttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);
        if (res.getStatusCode() != 202) {
            // Retries/error handling are out of scope for this ticket.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'ERP customer sync failed. Status: ' + res.getStatusCode() + ' Body: ' + res.getBody());
        }
    }

    private static String buildBody(List<Account> accounts) {
        JSONGenerator gen = JSON.createGenerator(false);
        gen.writeStartArray();
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            gen.writeStartObject();
            gen.writeStringField('salesforceId', String.valueOf(acc.Id));
            gen.writeStringField('name', acc.Name);
            if (String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry)) {
                gen.writeNullField('country');
            } else {
                gen.writeStringField('country', acc.BillingCountry);
            }
            gen.writeEndObject();
        }
        gen.writeEndArray();
        return gen.getAsString();
    }
}
```

**Deviation from the ticket: one request per account is not what this implements, and here is why.**

- **Governor limits.** A marketplace batch of 200 accounts would make 200 callouts in one transaction. The limit is 100. Accounts created one by one would also produce one HTTP call each, and the trigger would have to make a synchronous callout, which Salesforce doesn't allow.
- **The ERP API is built for arrays.** It accepts 1 to 200 customers per request. Sending one customer per call wastes calls and quota for no benefit.
- **Traceability is preserved.** Each customer in the array carries its own `salesforceId`, so the ERP team can still trace every account individually. Each request is capped at 200 customers, so a trigger invocation always becomes one request.

**Other design choices:**

- The trigger enqueues a Queueable with `Database.AllowsCallouts`. This moves the callout out of the transaction that performed the insert, so a failed ERP call cannot roll back the user's account creation.
- The job receives Ids, not sObjects, and re-queries the accounts. That gives it the committed field values.
- Customers are built with `JSONGenerator`, which writes an explicit JSON `null` for a blank `BillingCountry`.
- The job chunks lists at 200 defensively, though a single trigger invocation can't exceed 200 records.
- A non-202 response is logged with `System.debug`, since retries are out of scope.

I published the events in one `EventBus.publish` call instead of one call per opportunity. The returned list is index-aligned with the input, so you still know exactly which event failed. Per-record calls would add up to 200 publish calls per trigger transaction for no benefit. The other deviation is that `Stage__c` is truncated to its 40-character limit so a long stage name can't cause a publish failure.

